{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And behold, the plague has stayed in its eyes</b> — in its first appearance and its first measurement; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 13:5). Similarly, \"and its appearance is as the appearance of bdellium\" (Numbers 11:7), and likewise, \"like the appearance of the terrible ice\" (Ezekiel 1:22). But in Torat Kohanim (below, verse 37) we learned: I have only \"in its own eyes\" — from where do I derive \"in the eyes of his student\"? Scripture teaches (ibid.), \"and if in his eyes the eruption has stayed\" (Leviticus 13:37). If so, its meaning is: and if in the eyes of the priest mentioned, the plague has stood, that is to say it has remained standing in its place — it has not changed its location and has not spread — in the view of the eyes of the priest. And this is a familiar expression in the words of the Sages: \"it appears in my eyes,\" and likewise, \"and now let my life be precious in your eyes\" (II Kings 1:14) — in your knowledge and in your thought. And Scripture hints that the ruling concerning spreading is based on visual inspection — he need not measure the plague."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:21.438372788Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}